<!--
  Grype Container CVE Scan — Wazuh Decoder
  =========================================
  Processes per-finding JSON events written by the container-cve-scanner
  Ansible role (ESKA-174). Feeds the container_cve.xml rule set.

  Architecture
  ------------
  The container-cve-scanner role runs `grype <image> -o json` on each
  docker host, flattens each CVE match to a single JSON line, and appends
  it to /var/log/grype-findings.json. The Wazuh agent logcollector is
  configured to read that file with <log_format>json</log_format>.

  With json log format, Wazuh's built-in "json" parent decoder handles
  the initial parse — all top-level fields become dynamic extracted fields.
  No custom parent decoder is required. Rules scope to Grype events by
  matching on the integration="grype" field, which is unique to this source.

  Expected JSON format — one line per CVE finding
  ------------------------------------------------
  {
    "integration":       "grype",
    "timestamp":         "2024-01-15T10:30:00Z",
    "container_name":    "nginx",
    "image_name":        "nginx:1.25.3",
    "image_digest":      "sha256:abc123def456...",
    "cve_id":            "CVE-2023-44487",
    "severity":          "High",
    "package_name":      "libssl1.1",
    "package_version":   "1.1.1n-0+deb11u5",
    "fix_version":       "1.1.1n-0+deb11u6",
    "cvss_score":        "7.5"
  }

  Severity values (direct from Grype): Critical, High, Medium, Low,
  Negligible, Unknown.

  fix_version: set to a version string (e.g. "1.2.3") when a fix is
  available, or "none" / "not-fixed" when no fix exists.

  Deduplication
  -------------
  Dedup is handled upstream by the container-cve-scanner integration
  script, which maintains a per-host state file of previously-seen
  CVE+image+digest tuples. Only new findings are written to the log.
  Rules in container_cve.xml add a within-session suppressor (1-hour
  window) as a safety net for edge-case duplicate log entries.
-->
